  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe model the optimal function as a periodic-adaptive continuous wavelet, relaxing integer constraints and handling stability through dynamic repair. The central idea is to evolve the function's periodic behavior over the domain [0,2] using a reduced-dimensional representation with periodicity parameters. Our approach differs from previous methods by:\n1. Switching from real-valued to integer-indexed positions for better adaptability\n2. Incorporating local dynamic programming steps that construct solutions incrementally rather than random search\n3. Allowing continuous-period domain analysis (avoiding combinatorial explosion)\n4. Using adaptive repair that automatically shifts high/low regions to maintain integral constraint 80/2=40\nThis reduces the search space substantially and produces smoother transitions between high/low regions, hypothesized to minimize maximum overlap.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent the solution as a piecewise constant function h with N segments. Each segment is defined by:\n- Position xj \u2208 [0,2]\n- Height hj \u2208 [0,1]\n- Duration dj = dx (all segments equal width! fixed by dx=2/N)\nConstraints:\n\u2211 hj \u2248 40 (integral constraint)\nhj \u2208 {0,1} (binary values for N segments)\nOverlap integral C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx is minimized\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Setup:\n   - n_points = 80 (fixed)\n   - periodicity_domains = [0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8] (fixed trial periods)\n   - Defaults:\n        max_iter = 10000\n        repair_threshold = 3 (adjacency parameter)\n        max_period = 0.6\n\n2. Core algorithm steps:\n\n   A. Initial Configuration:\n      - If 'initial_h_values' exists:\n          Extract N_seg = len(initial_h_values)\n          Scale vector to domain [0,2] via uniform resizing\n          Adjust to sum\u224840 using linear restoration\n      - Else: Build random periodic structure:\n          random_start_vector(n_points, rng) augmented with repair steps\n\n   B. Optimization Loop:\n      For each iteration until time budget exhausted:\n      1. Determine trial periodicity period \u03c4 based on adaptive repair successes\n      2. Reshape function to satisfy integral by moving segment boundaries\n      3. Compute objective: correlation( h, 1-h ) / normalization\n      4. Dynamic program local modification (DP update) at optimal correlation point\n      5. Apply local repair using windowed down-sampling to propagate frequencies\n      6. Clipping: Ensure [0,1] domain with soft clipping (h[j] = clip(min(1, max(0, h[j])) and iterative repair\n\n   C. Repair Handler:\n      Inputs: failed_solution (vector), target_sum=40\n      Steps:\n        - Identify over/under-indexed regions\n        - Apply binary shift operations (0.1,0.2 thresholds)\n        - Introduce local inversion in heavily under/over regions\n        - Maintain global periodicity through cyclic adjustment\n\n   D. Final Cleaning:\n      - If final_sum deviates from 40+1e-6: use binary mirroring to adjust units\n\nSearch choices:\n- Reparameterization: instead of continuous parameters we index segments with fixed dx\n- Adaptive \u03c4 selection: based on repair success in previous windows\n- Probability thresholds: aggressive repair (70% down-sampling only near maxima)\n\nNumerical robustness:\n- Repair handles overflow naturally via segment reordering\n- Fall-back: 2-period base pattern if repair fails\n- Moving window ensures smooth transitions even after boundary crossings\n\nRuntime safeguards:\n- Monitors repair successes and escalates to new periodicity if stuck\n- Stochastic window shifting (each iteration \u226530% chance of temporal update)\n- Computing objective only for valid solutions (error repair first)\n\nReturn contract:\n- Always returns h_values, c5_bound, n_points\n- Enforces both constraint and sum\u224840 via repair\n- Objective computed only once after repair",
